[{:db/id 17592186331248 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/b946843e712c6294e261cb3015b02bc065cd265763805cf046009a2db0d11b83.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nTreat the drawing as schematic. As intended in the source worked solution, assume $D\\subseteq[-2,3]\\times[-2,2]$ and that $D$ contains points with $y>1$ and points with $x>2$. Require $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-1,3]\\times [-2,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "Use the explicit assumptions about the intended region, rather than estimating its boundary from the schematic drawing. Because $D$ has points with $y>1$, rectangle I, $[-1,3]\\times[-2,1]$, does not contain all of $D$. Because $D$ has points with $x>2$, rectangle II, $[-1,2]\\times[-2,2]$, also fails the containment requirement. The assumption $D\\subseteq[-2,3]\\times[-2,2]$ states exactly that rectangle III contains all of $D$.\n\nFor rectangle III, $f=e^{x+y}$ on $D$ and zero on $R\\setminus D$, so $\\iint_R f\\,dA=\\iint_De^{x+y}\\,dA$. Therefore the answer is \"III only\".\n\nThe original source solution states that rectangle III covers the region. The picture is schematic and its rendered boundary slightly crosses the $y=-2$ line; the study prompt makes the intended containing bounds explicit so the conclusion does not depend on those pixels." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
